add('C2','restoration','What the painting had become','sztuka museum kultura', '''
The portrait had hung in the town hall for so long that its brownish varnish seemed inseparable from its identity. Generations of residents knew the sitter as a severe, almost spectral figure emerging from a dim background. When a conservator proposed cleaning it, the trustees assumed that the task was straightforward: remove what time had added and recover what the painter had intended. The conservator, Amélie, was less comfortable with that formulation. Some layers were later additions, but the boundary between damage, historical alteration, and deliberate revision was not equally clear everywhere.

Tests revealed a bright blue sleeve beneath the yellowed surface and a small landscape that had all but disappeared. They also suggested that part of the sitter's hand had been repainted decades after the original work. Removing that paint might expose an earlier gesture, but it could also disturb fragile material beneath it. The available evidence did not establish whether the repainting had repaired a loss or changed the composition to suit a later owner's taste. An old photograph offered tantalizing support for both interpretations, depending on how its blurred shadows were read.

At a public meeting, several residents objected to the prospect of a more colorful portrait. They understood that the brown tone was not original, yet they felt that cleaning would erase the image through which the town had remembered its benefactor. Others dismissed this concern as sentimentality. Amélie resisted that dismissal without treating familiarity as a reason to preserve every accumulated layer. The painting was simultaneously a material object, an artwork with a history of interventions, and a civic image shaped by repeated encounters.

The trustees approved limited cleaning, retained the uncertain repainting, and commissioned a display explaining the choices. The blue sleeve reappeared; the hand remained as residents knew it. Some critics called the result indecisive, as if a coherent treatment had to move uniformly toward a single past moment. Amélie described it instead as a decision about the strength of the evidence in different parts of the same object.

The restored portrait looked both familiar and unexpectedly vivid. It did not reveal an uncontested original hiding intact beneath dirt. It made visible how much of what people called the original depended on judgments about which changes to reverse, which to retain, and which uncertainties future conservators should be allowed to reconsider.
''','''
What assumption did Amélie question?|That all later layers could be cleanly separated from a recoverable original|That the portrait was physically old|That varnish could change color|That residents recognized the sitter|the boundary between damage, historical alteration, and deliberate revision was not equally clear everywhere.|Warstwy nie dawały się wszędzie jednoznacznie sklasyfikować.
Why was the hand treated differently from the sleeve?|The evidence and risks differed between the two areas|The hand was not part of the painting|Blue paint was legally protected|Residents unanimously opposed any change|Removing that paint might expose an earlier gesture, but it could also disturb fragile material beneath it.|Niepewność interpretacji oraz ryzyko uszkodzenia uzasadniały inny zakres ingerencji.
How did Amélie respond to residents' attachment?|She treated it as relevant without making it an absolute veto|She agreed that nothing should ever be cleaned|She dismissed it as ignorance|She claimed the brown varnish was original|without treating familiarity as a reason to preserve every accumulated layer.|Uznała wagę znajomości obrazu, ale nie podporządkowała jej każdej decyzji.
What does the phrase 'a single past moment' criticize?|The idea that coherent restoration must return every part to one chosen date|The possibility of dating a painting|The residents' knowledge of local history|Any attempt to study earlier images|as if a coherent treatment had to move uniformly toward a single past moment.|Narrator kwestionuje utożsamienie spójności z jednolitym cofnięciem wszystkich zmian.
What was a benefit of retaining the uncertain repainting?|Future specialists could reconsider it|It proved who had repainted the hand|It eliminated every conservation risk|It made documentation unnecessary|which uncertainties future conservators should be allowed to reconsider.|Zachowanie warstwy pozostawia otwartą możliwość późniejszej oceny.
Which statement best captures the passage's view of restoration?|It involves evidence-based choices rather than simple recovery of a self-evident original|It should be determined entirely by public taste|It is impossible to improve any old artwork|It always requires complete removal of later paint|how much of what people called the original depended on judgments|Restauracja wymaga interpretacji materiału i oceny dowodów, a nie jedynie mechanicznego odsłonięcia.
''')
add('C2','exception','The exception that became the rule','technologia praca software', '''
The software company's reliability report attributed a recent outage to an exceptional sequence of events. A routine update had coincided with an unusually large import, after which an automatic recovery process had exhausted the remaining capacity. Each event, considered separately, fell within the system's documented limits. Together they produced a failure that no single team believed it owned. The report was precise about timing and conspicuously vague about responsibility.

An engineer named Dev accepted that the combination was uncommon. What he questioned was the inference that uncommon meant unforeseeable, or that unforeseeable meant nobody could have prepared for it. The teams had optimized their own components under assumptions about the behavior of neighboring components. Those assumptions were reasonable in isolation but had never been tested together under pressure. The recovery process, praised in earlier reviews for its persistence, kept retrying work that the overloaded system needed it to stop attempting.

Dev proposed a simulation in which several individually acceptable conditions occurred simultaneously. A manager worried that such tests would be expensive and could generate endless scenarios without a clear stopping point. Dev agreed that exhaustive prediction was impossible. His purpose was not to imagine every future accident, but to identify where the system lacked a way to fail gradually. A component that could temporarily refuse work might look less accommodating in a local performance chart while making the overall service more dependable.

The team eventually introduced limits on automatic retries and a controlled mode that postponed nonessential imports during overload. Customer support helped write messages explaining what was delayed and what remained available. In the next incident, some customers still experienced disruption, and the monthly report did not describe the outcome as flawless. The crucial difference was that the disruption remained contained rather than spreading until the entire service stopped.

At the review meeting, the phrase 'exceptional circumstances' appeared again. This time it introduced the conditions the system had handled imperfectly, not an argument for treating those conditions as outside its designers' concern. Dev regarded that linguistic change as significant, though hardly sufficient. An organization could adopt the vocabulary of resilience while continuing to reward only uninterrupted local performance. Whether the lesson endured would depend on what teams were allowed to sacrifice when the next set of targets was negotiated.
''','''
What is the implied criticism of the original report?|Technical precision coexisted with avoidance of shared responsibility|Its timeline was demonstrably false|It blamed a single customer without evidence|It contained no details about the failure|precise about timing and conspicuously vague about responsibility.|Kontrast sugeruje, że dokładność techniczna nie zastąpiła odpowiedzi na pytanie o odpowiedzialność.
What chain of assumptions did Dev challenge?|That rare events are necessarily unforeseeable and therefore beyond preparation|That every event can be predicted exactly|That all updates are inherently dangerous|That customers should never import data|uncommon meant unforeseeable, or that unforeseeable meant nobody could have prepared for it.|Dev rozdziela rzadkość, przewidywalność i możliwość ograniczania skutków.
Why had the recovery process become harmful?|Its repeated attempts consumed capacity during overload|It stopped after the first attempt|It refused all routine updates|It had never been praised|kept retrying work that the overloaded system needed it to stop attempting.|Zwykle ceniona uporczywość pogłębiła przeciążenie.
What was the aim of the proposed simulation?|To reveal weaknesses in containing failure|To predict every possible accident|To prove that local targets were always useless|To eliminate customer communication|identify where the system lacked a way to fail gradually.|Celem było kontrolowanie degradacji, a nie wszechwiedza o przyszłych awariach.
How did the next incident differ?|Disruption was limited instead of spreading across the service|No customer noticed any problem|All imports ran faster|The report avoided mentioning failures|the disruption remained contained rather than spreading|Poprawa polegała na ograniczeniu zasięgu, nie pełnym wyeliminowaniu zakłóceń.
What reservation does the final paragraph express?|New language matters little unless incentives allow the corresponding trade-offs|The vocabulary change guarantees future reliability|All performance targets must be abolished|Resilience means never refusing work|what teams were allowed to sacrifice when the next set of targets was negotiated.|Trwałość zmiany zależy od realnych zasad oceny i dopuszczalnych kompromisów.
''')
add('C2','guidebook','A guide to getting lost','podróż kultura travel', '''
The review copy arrived with a promise on its cover: a city guide that would help visitors escape the itinerary. Its author recommended unnamed alleys, unadvertised courtyards, and cafes supposedly untouched by tourism. The reviewer, Celia, found the photographs beautiful and the premise faintly self-defeating. Once a secluded courtyard appeared in a widely distributed guide, its seclusion became something the book was both celebrating and helping to undo. This did not make the recommendation dishonest, but it complicated the claim that readers could purchase an unmediated encounter with the city.

Celia followed one suggested walk. The route led through a residential district where a baker greeted regular customers by name and a delivery driver temporarily blocked the narrow street. Nothing dramatic happened. At the recommended cafe, she met three people carrying the same guide. They laughed at the coincidence, then lowered their voices as though their shared wish to avoid tourists required them to conceal their own presence. Celia wondered whether the book had offered escape from tourism or merely a more flattering role within it.

Yet she was reluctant to turn the review into a performance of superiority. Without the guide, she would probably never have visited that district, and the cafe owner seemed pleased with the extra customers. Nor did the residents exist to supply outsiders with an atmosphere of authentic everyday life. A place was not spoiled simply because a stranger learned how to reach it. The difficulty lay in the expectation that it should remain unchanged for the benefit of successive visitors who each imagined themselves unusually perceptive.

Her review praised the practical maps and criticized the rhetoric of discovery. The guide was useful when it admitted to being a guide; it became less convincing when it presented its own recommendations as a release from recommendation itself. Celia suggested reading it as an invitation to pay attention, not as a certificate that one's experiences were more genuine than those of people on a sightseeing bus.

The publisher quoted the phrase 'an invitation to pay attention' in its publicity, leaving out the qualification. Celia was irritated, then amused. Her carefully balanced review had supplied another compact promise for the cover. The city would continue changing regardless, but the appetite for instructions on how to encounter it without instructions appeared remarkably durable.
''','''
Why does Celia find the premise self-defeating?|Publicizing secluded places can alter the quality being advertised|The maps contain no streets|The author refuses to recommend cafes|All visitors dislike quiet districts|its seclusion became something the book was both celebrating and helping to undo.|Popularny poradnik może osłabiać odosobnienie, które poleca jako zaletę.
What is implied by the visitors lowering their voices?|They feel an awkward tension between their self-image and their shared tourist behavior|The owner has ordered absolute silence|They are planning to leave without paying|They cannot understand one another|their shared wish to avoid tourists required them to conceal their own presence.|Gest pokazuje napięcie między byciem turystą a pragnieniem odróżnienia się od turystów.
Why does Celia resist simply condemning the guide?|It has practical value and residents are not obliged to remain unchanged|She believes the publisher will pay her more|She thinks all advertised claims are accurate|She dislikes the cafe owner|A place was not spoiled simply because a stranger learned how to reach it.|Nie utożsamia odkrycia miejsca przez gości z automatycznym zniszczeniem jego wartości.
What distinction underlies her review?|Practical guidance differs from a claim to certify authentic experience|Maps are always superior to photographs|Local cafes should never accept strangers|All independent travel is better than bus travel|an invitation to pay attention, not as a certificate|Poradnik może pomagać obserwować, ale nie uprawnia do uznawania własnych przeżyć za bardziej autentyczne.
What is ironic about the publisher's quotation?|A criticism of simplified promises becomes another simplified promise|The publisher quotes a sentence not in the review|Celia had demanded that every word be removed|The quotation makes the guide unusable|Her carefully balanced review had supplied another compact promise for the cover.|Wycięty z kontekstu komplement powiela mechanizm, który recenzja problematyzowała.
Which description best fits Celia's stance?|Critical but aware that she is implicated in the same contradictions|Unreservedly hostile to every guidebook|Certain that she alone travels authentically|Indifferent to the residents' interests|she was reluctant to turn the review into a performance of superiority.|Samokrytycyzm chroni ją przed przedstawieniem siebie jako wyjątku od opisywanego zjawiska.
''')
add('C2','unwritten-contract','The unwritten contract','biznes praca community', '''
For nearly twenty years, a small bakery had supplied bread to the community hall at a price barely above cost. There was no written agreement. The arrangement had begun when the hall's former caretaker helped the bakery survive a flood, and both parties had gradually come to regard it as ordinary. New volunteers knew the discount existed but not how it had originated. The baker, Tomas, rarely mentioned the history; he was proud of the connection and uncomfortable making generosity sound like a debt.

When flour and energy costs rose, Tomas told the hall's new treasurer that he would need to increase the price. She compared his proposed rate with two larger suppliers and found it still competitive, though no longer exceptionally cheap. At the committee meeting, however, several volunteers described the increase as a withdrawal of support. One suggested that the bakery had benefited for years from the hall's recommendations and therefore owed it a continued concession. Tomas, who had not been invited to that discussion, heard the remark through a customer.

His first impulse was to end the arrangement. What hurt was not the negotiation over price but the transformation of something he had chosen to give into something others claimed the right to demand. The treasurer recognized a different problem. She had inherited a budget dependent on an informal subsidy whose conditions nobody had documented. Treating the old price as permanent had made the hall's finances appear more secure than they were. Neither the bakery nor the volunteers had deliberately concealed this fragility, but everyone had found it convenient not to name it.

She invited Tomas to meet two committee members. They agreed on a price reviewed annually, with a small additional donation from the bakery for one particular event. The distinction mattered: ordinary purchases would be paid for as purchases, and support would be described as support. Some volunteers regretted the loss of an arrangement that had seemed warmer precisely because it was unwritten. Tomas shared a little of that regret, yet he no longer wanted warmth to depend on his absorbing costs that others did not see.

The new agreement did not exhaust the relationship. Tomas still sent extra rolls when an event attracted more people than expected, and the volunteers still recommended his shop. What changed was the presumption that kindness, once repeated often enough, ceased to be a choice. Putting part of the relationship in writing had not necessarily diminished trust; it had removed one way in which trust could quietly become obligation.
''','''
Why had the original arrangement become vulnerable to misunderstanding?|Its history and conditions were no longer shared knowledge|The bakery had always charged the highest price|The hall had a detailed permanent contract|Tomas had publicly demanded repayment after the flood|New volunteers knew the discount existed but not how it had originated.|Znano cenę, ale nie okoliczności ani warunki powstania ustalenia.
What particularly hurt Tomas?|A voluntary contribution was being treated as an entitlement|The treasurer compared suppliers|The hall had stopped buying bread years earlier|His new price was above every competitor's|something he had chosen to give into something others claimed the right to demand.|Uraziło go roszczeniowe przeformułowanie dobrowolnego gestu, nie sama rozmowa o kosztach.
What financial weakness did the treasurer identify?|The budget relied on an undocumented subsidy assumed to be permanent|The hall had no customers at any event|The bakery was charging twice the agreed rate|The volunteers had deliberately falsified accounts|a budget dependent on an informal subsidy whose conditions nobody had documented.|Nieformalna pomoc została potraktowana jak stabilny element finansowania.
Why was the separate event donation significant?|It distinguished a commercial price from a chosen act of support|It restored the old discount on every order|It made annual reviews unnecessary|It required Tomas to donate indefinitely|ordinary purchases would be paid for as purchases, and support would be described as support.|Nazwanie dwóch rodzajów relacji zapobiega myleniu zapłaty z darowizną.
How is the nostalgia for the unwritten arrangement qualified?|Its apparent warmth concealed costs borne unevenly|All volunteers had knowingly exploited Tomas|Written agreements prevent any generosity|Tomas had never valued the connection|warmth to depend on his absorbing costs that others did not see.|Tekst uznaje emocjonalną wartość dawnej relacji, ale pokazuje niewidoczny koszt po jednej stronie.
What does the ending suggest about formal agreements?|They can protect voluntary generosity by clarifying obligations|They always replace trust entirely|They guarantee that costs will never rise|They eliminate the need for communication|removed one way in which trust could quietly become obligation.|Jasne obowiązki mogą wspierać zaufanie, zamiast je automatycznie osłabiać.
''')

